## GATE-alias. the cold oracle was measuring past the end of its own ruler — FIXED 2026-08-25
THE FIRST DIAGNOSIS IN THIS FILE WAS WRONG, and the way it was wrong is the lesson. It said the
click↔flash PAIRING was ambiguous on a uniform 1 s grid and that the estimator was picking the wrong
alignment. Both halves sounded right and neither was the cause. What settled it was making the rig
dump the failing run instead of arguing about the summary line — the numbers, verbatim, from a cold
run that failed at −438.7 ms:
    beep arrivals   1537, 2537, 3537, 4537, 5537   residual vs k·1000 = 537 on EVERY one
    flash arrivals  1019, 2020, 3020, 4020, 5020   residual = 19-20
    export onsets   1.395 …   export flashes 0.833 …   true offset = +562 ms
THE AUDIOCONTEXT STARTUP STALL WAS 537 ms — perfectly regular, trivially measurable, and past TWO
hard limits at once:
  (1) estimateScheduleSkewFromArrivals refused it (MAX_SCHEDULE_STALL_MS = 450), so there was no
      correction at all and the gate fell to its RAW rung — which is why the failure line said
      `(raw)` and why three sessions of theorising about aliasing missed it;
  (2) the pairing window is half the grid wide and centred on ZERO, so +562 is not representable and
      came back as its complement against the next flash, −438.
So the run was not out of sync. The ruler was too short and nothing said so.
FOUR FIXES, each measured:
  · THE STALL IS ARITHMETIC, NOT A SEARCH. A beep is SCHEDULED at k·interval and can only render
    LATE — an AudioContext that has not started cannot make a sound early. So arrival = k·interval +
    stall with stall ∈ [0, interval), k = floor(arrival / interval), and the whole candidate-index
    sweep and its 450 ms clamp go away. Missed first beep still works (k moves, the relation does
    not). The magnitude clamps in isAliasedScheduleCorrection went with it: what distinguishes an
    alias from a stall is not size, it is whether the raw reading AGREES (a real stall is in the raw
    offset too) — the clause that caught every historical flake, and it stays.
  · THE PAIRING IS TOLD WHERE TO LOOK. The rig measures both reference schedules before it decodes
    anything, and their difference is where a correctly-paired export must land. flashSyncStats takes
    that expectation and centres its window there; omitted, it is the old behaviour exactly.
  · THE PAIRING PICKS BY CONSENSUS, NOT BY COUNT. The old sweep kept whichever alignment produced the
    MOST pairs. Injection-proven red: with a doubled detector it reports −400 ms where the truth is
    +50. Now every (onset, flash) pair proposes an offset and the one most events agree on wins, ties
    to the smaller |offset| — the band is ±90 ms against a 1000 ms grid, so "a whole second out" needs
    more evidence than "in band", not less.
  · THE RIG WARMS ITS ENCODER, BECAUSE PRODUCTION DOES (note 6's fourth instance). A Chrome PROCESS's
    first VideoEncoder pays a multi-second init and every oracle run is a fresh process; production
    pays it at MOUNT (prearm.ts → encoderWarm.ts) while this rig paid it DURING the take. On a 6 s
    take that is the whole take: two dumped failures show the render finding all five flashes while
    the packet-copied exports found one and none, so inst/trim sync could not be computed — and the
    run PASSED, because the band was written `typeof mean === 'number'`.
TWO VACUITY HOLES CLOSED WITH IT, and the second one caught a real run within minutes: a fast path
that RAN but produced no number now fails instead of passing silently, on both the instant and the
trimmed path.
ONE SAFETY NET, for the case the arithmetic still cannot see: a stall of a FULL interval wraps
silently (1100 ms reads as 100). Nothing in the signal can distinguish that, so the gate refuses a run
whose stall exceeds 0.75 × the grid. Measured headroom: ~130 ms before the rig warmed its encoder,
326-531 ms after, so the threshold is real headroom and not a hair trigger.
MEASURED AFTER, 22 cold runs across three batches: ZERO sync failures, zero aliasing, zero `n/a`
fast-path numbers, and every run on the `metric=symmetric` rung (before: raw, corrected and n/a
scattered across runs). sync 36.6-65.4 ms, instant 41.8-78.5, smart cut 41.3-78.8.
TRIED AND DELETED: a 750 ms settle between the warm and the take, on the theory that the warm and the
AudioContext were competing for the machine. Eight cold runs with it (stall 326-498) against eight
without (343-462) say they are not. The warm does raise the stall ~3x and that is simply its price.
STILL OPEN, and it is NOT this: the SPUR band fails about one cold run in seven at −33 to −38 dB
against a −40 band, on a settled machine as well as a busy one (1 of 6 idle, 2 of 8 under load). That
is the known capture-starvation artifact STATE already describes for the fidelity oracle, it predates
this work, and it is the only remaining cold-gate flake. It wants its own session.
WHAT THE INSTRUMENT STILL CANNOT DO, named so the next attempt starts here: the beep grid is uniform,
so a stall beyond one full interval is undetectable rather than merely unrepresentable. The structural
answer is a NON-UNIFORM or coded beep schedule, which makes the index unique at any stall. Not built —
the arithmetic above covers everything measured so far, and a coded schedule changes the content every
historical number was taken against.

## P0-instant-sync. the packet-copy paths were on the wrong clock — DONE 2026-08-25, merged
WHAT IT WAS, and it is a CONTRACT hole rather than a bug in either path: the composite is a second
file with its own clock, and that clock does not start when the take does. v1's file begins when its
MediaRecorder begins — after the <video> elements, the canvas, the audio graph and the durable write
stream have all been built (measured 134-215 ms into the take); v2's begins at whichever of audio or
video first reached the worker (66-90 ms). CompositeRecording had no field that could express this, so
instant.ts and smartCut.ts both assumed composite time IS recording time and placed copied video
against audio mixed from the raw channels on the recording timeline.
SHIPPED:
  · CompositeRecording.startOffsetMs — "composite t=0 is recording time startOffsetMs", the same
    sentence ChannelRecording.startOffsetMs already makes for a channel. ABSENT means 0, i.e. the old
    assumption preserved for takes recorded before the field existed (nothing can recover their origin
    now, and a guess would be worse than the behaviour they were exported with).
  · both engines produce it: the v2 worker reports its origin as a MAIN-THREAD stamp
    (CompositorStats.originAtMs — every arrival is stamped there, so the subtraction is between two
    readings of one clock); v1 stamps the instant after recorder.start(). session.ts passes the epoch
    and REBASES the composite by the same minOffset the channels get. The oracle rig does both too —
    it drives the engines directly, so wiring production alone would have left the gate blind.
  · v1's durationMs is now measured from that same instant instead of from before the recorder
    existed. Total output length is unchanged (offset + duration = stop − epoch either way); what
    moves is where the video sits inside it.
  · compose/compositeTime.ts — the four-line conversion, pure, unit-tested, so the decision has one
    home and a test rather than being re-derived at each call site.
  · instant.ts places every copied packet at packet.timestamp + offset and mixes audio over the whole
    take; smartCut.ts maps each kept span through the offset (negative composite time = the span
    begins before the composite's first frame, which is a real hole in it and NOT a reason to slide
    its first frame backwards).
MEASURED (symmetric flash+click, the same metric `sync=` uses):
    v2   inst 185.1 ms BEFORE → 48.6 / 59.6 / 75.8 ms after · same takes' render 50.0 / 64.2 / 54.8
    v1   inst 244.8 ms before → 55.7 / 57.8 ms          · same takes' render 61.7 / 59.5
    the declared offsets on those runs: v2 66-90 ms, v1 134-215 ms
GATED NOW (the previous session deliberately left it measured-but-ungated): scripts/oracle-gate.mjs
bands the instant path at ≤90 ms symmetric, mean and maxAbs, PLUS the anti-vacuity check that the
unedited export really took 'instant' — a silently declining copy path fails the run instead of
passing it on the render's numbers. `compOff=` joins `inst=`/`compT0=` on every oracle line.
TWO THINGS THE FIX ALSO SETTLED:
  (1) THE "ONE FRAME AHEAD" OFFSET WAS THIS, not compositing. Three sessions attributed it to the
      composite being built from source frames as they arrive; `npm run exp -- o5cut` now reports
      compositeOffsetFrames 0 where it read +1, with the same rig and the same take shape.
  (2) THE FAST PATHS AND `sync=` WERE READ ON DIFFERENT METRICS — inst/trim on the audio-corrected
      number, sync= on the symmetric one, a ~13.5 ms systematic between two figures printed side by
