## P0-tail. v1 LOSES THE TAIL UNDER LOAD — FIXED 2026-08-23, merged (evidence in DECISIONS)
WAS: on a 4K source the shipped composite delivered 13.3 fps and its last decodable frame sat 3769 ms
before the end of the take (re-measured at the start of the fix session: 2734 ms on a 10 s take).
"Loom cuts last seconds, we don't do that shit" — happening in our own engine.
FIX, in the order the evidence forced:
  1. TIMESLICE 1000 -> 250 ms. 2734 -> 675/922 ms on its own.
  2. STOP PAINTING BEFORE STOPPING. The compositor kills its tick, then lets the encoder work.
  3. PROBE THE ENCODER with requestData() instead of waiting for the byte flow to go quiet. This is
     the fix: under load the recorder emits FOUR chunks in ten seconds, so "no bytes for 200 ms" is
     true immediately and proves nothing (that version measured 150/198/579 ms — better, and wrong).
     A requestData() flushes what has been encoded, so an EMPTY answer is real evidence.
MEASURED at 4K, 10 s takes: tail 43, 56, 167, 320 ms — all inside O8's 400 ms band, with ~1.0-1.1 MB
per take recovered by the drain that used to be discarded. Healthy 1080p: tail 71/80 ms (was 65-76 —
unchanged), delivered 29.7/29.9 fps (unchanged), drain costs 242 ms at stop and recovers 0 bytes,
because there is nothing to recover.
(a) THE GATE CAN NOW SEE IT: `npm run oracle:load` (scripts/oracle-load.mjs) runs the band under the
4K load and exits non-zero when it is red. HEAVY — announce it, never run it under the PO's feet, and
it is deliberately NOT in the pre-push hook. Proven red before the fix by the same harness (675/922 ms
with the drain disabled, 2734 ms as shipped); reproduce by setting DRAIN_BUDGET_MS = 0.
(b) INSTRUMENTED: LiveCompositeStats (frames painted, chunks, drain ms, bytes recovered, timed out) in
one console line at every stop, warn-level when the drain timed out.
(c) DECIDED, per the precedent F1 and the liveness work set: a composite whose drain TIMED OUT is
marked CompositeRecording.tailIncomplete, and exportInstant refuses to packet-copy it — the export
falls back to the full render from the raw channels. Correctness over instant. Note the signal is
conservative: it means "the encoder never caught up in 2 s", not "the tail is short by >400 ms",
because nothing cheap can measure the latter at stop.
FOUND ALONGSIDE, and it is now task P0-tail-raw: the RAW channels lose tail too. Same rig, same take:
203-753 ms depending on load. Two caveats keep it off P0: the rig records the UNCAPPED 4K source
