THE IDEA, as P0-instant-sync left it: the composite's two ends are read off different clocks — the
   file's origin is the first audio batch's CAPTURE time, while every video frame is stamped
   `performance.now()` when the reader hands it over, an ARRIVAL time late by the pipeline delay. A
   VideoFrame carries its own `timestamp`, the capture side of the same event, so stamp frames with
   that instead.
WHAT THE RIG FOUND (`npm run exp -- o4clock`, new; 20 s, both sources, canvas capture tracks —
   getUserMedia is unreachable headless without a fake device, and the run says so):
     lane            frames   mean d    sd    mean excess   p95 excess   drift
     canvas-screen    1201    486.1   1.04       2.74         4.05      −26 ppm  (−23.3 ms / 900 s)
     canvas-camera     601    487.2   1.00       2.97         4.10      −20 ppm  (−18.1 ms / 900 s)
   d = arrival − frame.timestamp. THREE THINGS FOLLOW AND THEY KILL IT:
   (1) `firstTimestampMs` is 0 — the frame clock's EPOCH IS TAKE-RELATIVE, not performance.now()'s.
       So d's absolute value is meaningless and only its shape can be used. The best a min-filter can
       do is `timestamp + min d`, whose zero is the FIRST frame's arrival — which is itself late by
       exactly the delivery latency we wanted to remove. The absolute latency is not recoverable from
       this signal at all; the browser exposes no capture-time reference for a local video track (the
       audio path's `track.getSettings().latency` has no video equivalent).
   (2) What IS recoverable is the JITTER, and there is 2.7-3.0 ms of it (4.1 ms at p95). The
       instrument's own sync noise floor is ~8-9 ms 1σ (note 1), so this is not measurable on the
       gate even if it were free.
   (3) It is not free. The media clock runs −20 to −26 ppm against the wall clock, so a whole-take
       minimum would trade a 3 ms fixed error for 18-23 ms of DRIFT on a 900 s take — and PO records
       938-1800 s. A sliding-window minimum bounds that, and was written and unit-tested, but it is
       eight fenced files-worth of capture-path risk for ≤3 ms nobody can measure.
   AND THE RESIDUAL ITSELF IS NOT JITTER, provably: the oracle rig uses these same canvas sources,
   where delivery jitter is 1 ms sd, and it still reads the instant path 25-33 ms above the render.
WHAT WOULD ACTUALLY CLOSE IT, named for the next session: the composite and the raw channels need to
   share ONE media clock. Today the composite is placed on wall-clock arrivals and the raw channels
   on MediaRecorder's own media timeline, and the gap between those two is the residual. That
   converges when we own the raw channel's encoder too — i.e. it is O3b's, not O4-polish's.
